/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The foetal heart is small and sits inside a moving mother.
   - Abdominal ECG uses electrodes on the mother's abdomen.
   - FHR is the foetal heart rate in beats per minute; Tf is the time between two foetal beats in seconds.
   - A direct foetal scalp electrode is a small clip placed on the foetal scalp after the membranes have ruptured and the cervix is dilated.
   - An intrauterine pressure catheter lies in the uterus and measures pressure.
   - Foetal phonocardiography uses a microphone on the abdomen.
   - An arrhythmia monitor watches one ECG lead.
   - Rinst is the instantaneous heart rate in beats per minute; RR is the interval between two adjacent R waves in seconds.
   - A monitor stores limits.
   - Artefact rejection checks QRS shape, a refractory period and the previous RR; a very short RR after a normal beat may be noise.
*/
