/* book Worked example: Q: A cardiotocograph on a mother at 38 weeks shows a baseline foetal heart rate of 140 beats per minute with variability of 5 beats per minute (the lower end of the normal 5 to 25 range). After one contraction the rate falls to 90 beats per minute; the fall starts 20 s after the contraction begins and its lowest point comes 30 s after the contraction peak. (a) Name the deceleration and its usual cause. (b) How long is one foetal beat interval at baseline? (c) In another ward an adult arrhythmia monitor with a 1.5 s pause limit measures RR = 1.60 s. What rate does it report, and which alarm sounds
   1. (a) The fall starts after the contraction has begun and its lowest point lags the contraction peak, so it is a late deceleration. Late decelerations point to poor placental blood flow.
   2. (b) Tf = 60/140 = 0.4286 s = 428.6 ms.
   3. (c) Rate = 60/1.60 = 37.5 beats per minute. 1.60 s is longer than the 1.5 s limit, so the pause alarm sounds; check the strip for a missed QRS before treating it as a true pause.
   Answer: (a) Late deceleration (placental insufficiency). (b) About 429 ms. (c) 37.5 beats per minute; the pause alarm sounds. */
