/* book Worked example 2: Q: A 2.0 MHz foetal Doppler probe looks straight along the motion of a foetal heart wall moving at 0.15 m/s (c = 1540 m/s). (a) Find the Doppler shift. (b) What shift does the same wall give if the beam meets its motion at 60°?
   1. (a) fd = 2 f0 v cos θ / c = 2 × 2.0 × 10⁶ × 0.15 × 1 / 1540 = 6.0 × 10⁵ / 1540 = 390 Hz.
   2. (b) cos 60° = 0.5, so fd = 390 × 0.5 = 195 Hz.
   3. Both shifts are audible, which is why the monitor can play the foetal heart as a sound; a poorly aimed probe gives a weaker, lower shift and the rate counter may drop beats.
   Answer: (a) About 390 Hz. (b) About 195 Hz at 60°. */
