/* book exam traps: A large regular QRS at about 70 to 90 beats per minute on an abdominal ECG is often the mother's. Do not read it as foetal. Foetal rate is 110 to 160 beats per minute and needs maternal QRS cancellation. | One missed foetal beat doubles one RR interval and halves the rate calculated from that interval. The monitor is not measuring a true bradycardia. | Early deceleration starts with the contraction; late begins after the contraction starts, with its nadir after the peak; variable is abrupt and changes shape. The examiner often gives the timing and asks for the name. | Baseline rate and beat-to-beat variability are different. A baseline of 140 beats per minute can still have reduced variability, which is a warning sign. | Pause is one long RR interval; bradycardia is a sustained low rate over several beats. Check whether the alarm limit is in seconds or beats per minute. */
